<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of British Columbia (1858-1866)</h1><p class="st-meta">1858 CE – 1866 CE · capital New Westminster</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mainland British colony created in haste in 1858 to control the tens of thousands of miners drawn by the Fraser River gold rush.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When gold was found on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraser-river">Fraser River</a> in 1858, some 30,000 miners, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, poured into territory held only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> posts. To assert British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, Parliament created the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> in August 1858. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor">James Douglas</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-vancouver-island">Colony of Vancouver Island</a>, also became its governor after cutting his ties with the Company.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Royal Engineers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-clement-moody">Richard Clement Moody</a> surveyed the land and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-westminster">New Westminster</a> as capital in 1859. Judge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-baillie-begbie">Matthew Baillie Begbie</a> enforced law in the mining camps. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cariboo">Cariboo</a> gold rush from 1862 led to the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cariboo-wagon-road">Cariboo Wagon Road</a>. Miners' violence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nlakapamux">Nlaka'pamux</a> villages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraser-canyon-war">Fraser Canyon War</a> of 1858 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsilhqotin-war">Tsilhqot'in War</a> of 1864 marked relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nations-in-canada">First Nations</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor">Douglas</a> retired in 1864 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-seymour">Frederick Seymour</a>. Costly road building left heavy debts as gold output fell, and in 1866 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> united the mainland with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-vancouver-island">Vancouver Island</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1866-1871">Colony of British Columbia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1858 CE – 1866 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.68%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1858 CE – 1866 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Westminster</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Douglas, Frederick Seymour</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Columbia (mainland colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1862&amp;focus=colony-of-british-columbia-1858-1866" aria-label="Colony of British Columbia (1858-1866) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1846 462 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of British Columbia (1858-1866) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2166 718L4997 651M2166 718L2166 718"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M2166 718h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2166 718h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2166 718h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks New Westminster. <a href="/atlas?year=1862&amp;focus=colony-of-british-columbia-1858-1866">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
